From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] WM: respect landscape requests on a naturally landscape
 display

A large screen ignores fixed-orientation requests by default
(DisplayContent.getIgnoreOrientationRequest()): the display follows the
sensor or the user's rotation lock, and an app whose request does not
match is letterboxed. On a tablet used mostly in landscape this keeps
portrait-only phone apps from turning the display to portrait, but it
also letterboxes landscape-only apps, most games, whenever the display
is in portrait when they start: the app is shown as a landscape strip
at the top of the display until the user turns the tablet.

On a display whose natural orientation is landscape, respect requests
for landscape (landscape, sensorLandscape, reverseLandscape and
userLandscape) in DisplayArea.shouldIgnoreOrientationRequest(). Other
requests are still ignored unless exempted per app, so portrait-only
apps stay letterboxed in landscape. A landscape-only app turns the
display to landscape, as it does where orientation requests are not
ignored. Tasks that cannot specify an orientation (multi-window, split
screen) are unaffected, and the per-app aspect ratio settings and the
other app-compat treatments still see ignoreOrientationRequest enabled.
As on displays that do not ignore requests, a window that sets a
landscape screenOrientation in its layout params is respected too.

"landscape" and "reverseLandscape" each give one fixed rotation
(DisplayRotation.rotationForOrientation()), so a tablet held the other
way round would be turned over; players that pick between the two from
their own sensor listener would turn it back and forth. Rotate for
"userLandscape" instead wherever the display computes a rotation from
them (the display orientation, the fixed rotation of a launching or a
non-top visible activity, the snapshot check of a trampoline): the
sensor or the rotation lock chooses between both landscape rotations,
as they do while the display is in landscape and the request is
ignored. The requests themselves are not changed, so apps still read
back what they asked for.

A translucent or floating activity whose request is ignored, such as a
portrait login dialog over a landscape game, now takes its orientation
from the activities behind it, as if it requested "behind", also where
the display looks for a top activity that follows the one behind and
where it applies a fixed rotation to non-top visible activities.
Otherwise its request ends the search, the display follows the sensor
or the rotation lock to portrait, and the game behind it is laid out in
portrait: its own request is respected, so it is not letterboxed, and
as the display has no orientation source no fixed rotation is applied
to it. Not while the keyguard is locked, where the activities behind
may be hidden.

Conversely, a translucent activity that requests landscape is now an
orientation source. A letterboxed activity behind it whose request is
ignored no longer gets a fixed rotation for that request, which would
draw it sideways and lay it out again; an activity that is not
letterboxed, typically one that filled the portrait display now rotating
for the top, still keeps its appearance through the transform.

Activity Embedding: TaskFragments shown side by side report
"unspecified", so the display follows the sensor. A split that is shown
only in landscape is stacked in portrait, where its containers fill the
task and the task takes the orientation of the first one, from the top,
whose activities report one. A landscape request from it now rotates the
display to landscape, which shows the split side by side; the display
would then follow the sensor back to portrait, stack the split, and
start over. While the display is in landscape, embedded TaskFragments
shown side by side report "userLandscape" instead when a walk over them
from the top, as the task walks them stacked (skipping those that report
nothing or "behind"), first meets a landscape request, and make the
requesting activity the orientation source, so that a rotation lock does
not take it for a system request. Requests below an opaque container are
covered when the split is stacked, so they do not keep the display in
landscape. A placeholder that is finished in portrait uncovers its
primary; WM Jetpack launches no placeholder beside a primary whose
container requests landscape and dismisses one already shown ("WM
Jetpack: parallel view from the app's EasyGo rules"). The root tasks of
split screen are adjacent too and keep reporting "unspecified".

Opening Overview from a landscape app while the sensor or the rotation
lock says portrait puts the display rotation into the transition that
starts recents; this needs "WM Shell: cancel recents when its starting
transition rotates the display", without which only the wallpaper stays
on screen.

Seen on a 2560x1600 tablet: a game requesting "landscape", started
with the tablet held in portrait, was shown 1600x1000 at the top of the
portrait display.

